WebSockets are commonly used when you need real-time communication between client
and server (chat apps, live notifications, stock prices, live dashboards, etc.).
 As a Java developer preparing for interviews, you should know both concept and Spring Boot implementation.

1. What is WebSocket?

WebSocket is a protocol that provides full-duplex communication between client and server over a single persistent TCP connection.

Normal HTTP:

Client → Request
Server → Response
Connection closed

WebSocket:

Client ↔ Server (continuous two-way communication)
Connection stays open

So server can push data to client anytime without client requesting.

Example:

Chat application

Live stock price

Notifications

Multiplayer games

2. How WebSocket Connection Starts

1️⃣ Client sends HTTP request to upgrade connection.

GET /chat
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade

2️⃣ Server accepts upgrade.

HTTP 101 Switching Protocols

3️⃣ Now connection becomes WebSocket.

Client ↔ Server

Both can send messages anytime.

3. WebSocket in Spring Boot

Spring Boot supports WebSockets using STOMP protocol.

Dependency:

<dependency>
 <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
 <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-websocket</artifactId>
</dependency>

4. WebSocket Configuration
@Configuration
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

    @Override
    public void registerStompEndpoints(StompEndpointRegistry registry) {
        registry.addEndpoint("/chat")
                .setAllowedOrigins("*")
                .withSockJS();
    }

    @Override
    public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry registry) {
        registry.enableSimpleBroker("/topic");
        registry.setApplicationDestinationPrefixes("/app");
    }
}

Explanation:

/chat      → connection endpoint
/topic     → message subscription
/app       → message sending prefix

5. Controller Example
@Controller
public class ChatController {

    @MessageMapping("/send")
    @SendTo("/topic/messages")
    public String sendMessage(String message) {
        return message;
    }
}

Flow:

Client send → /app/send
Server process
Server broadcast → /topic/messages
All subscribed clients receive

6. JavaScript Client Example
var socket = new SockJS('/chat');
var stompClient = Stomp.over(socket);

stompClient.connect({}, function() {

    stompClient.subscribe('/topic/messages', function(message){
        console.log(message.body);
    });

    stompClient.send("/app/send", {}, "Hello Server");
});

